  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe supplied solution uses coarse grid optimization followed by fine grid optimization. This method can get trapped in local optima and is sensitive to initial conditions. \nOur approach uses a multi-start strategy with a population-based optimization algorithm (differential evolution) to explore the search space more effectively. We also incorporate analytical structures inspired by Golomb ruler constructions to generate high-quality initial solutions.\n\nKey improvements:\n- Differential evolution for global search\n- Patterned initialization with peak spreads to cover multiple structures\n- Adaptive dimension reduction when time is limited\n- Quick validation of candidate solutions \n\nThis approach systematically explores different hump configurations, significantly increasing the chances of finding improved upper bounds.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Variable: h, a step function with n_points samples over [0,2]; dx=2/n_points\n- Objective: C5 = (1/n_points) * max_k (integral_{x=0}^{2-dk} h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx) for all k\n- Formally: C5 = (1/n_points) * max_k (np.correlate(h, 1-h, 'full')[k] for appropriate k\n- Constraint: \u2211h[i] * dx =1, i.e., \u2211h = n_points/2 (approximately)\n- Representation: h stored as an array of length n_points with values in [0,1]\n\nThe optimization will take advantage of analytical pattern characteristics and ensure all candidates satisfy the integral constraint. We use differential evolution with parameters tailored for efficiency.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n- n_points set to default 280 (initial parent's resolution)\n- Generate multiple diverse initial solutions:\n    1. Centered hump at mid-interval [1-0.5*spread, 1+0.5*spread] with intensity >0.5\n    2. Dual-hump solution (Golomb-inspired) with two peaks\n    3. Perturbations of parent solution if available\n- Use differential evolution with:\n    - Bounds: [0,1] for each coefficient\n    - n_pop = 30\n    - maxiter = 100 (higher would be too slow)\n- Optimization objectives:\n    - Minimize (1/i) * max_k(corr[k]) for various k, with proper weighting\n- Include early termination if time budget exceeded\n- Ensure all candidates satisfy \u2211h[i] \u2248 n_points/2 with variation <0.5%\n- Final scoring uses exact correlation calculation\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n- Fundamental variation: Patterned initialization with adjustable hump widths\n- Supports multiple modes: Centered, dual-hump, and random-like\n- Automatically adapts to available time through iterative rounds\n- Fallback to parent solution if no better found\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Dominant cost: 280-dimensional DE evaluations ~ 30\u00d7100\u00d7n_points correlation calculations\n- Each correlation: O(n_points log(n_points)) via FFT\n- Time Estimate: Under 100 seconds for full run\n- Early termination at any point\n- Reprojection of solutions to satisfy integral constraint\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- For every candidate:\n  - Check element bounds: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n  - Check sum: |\u2211h[i] - n_points/2|/ (n_points/2) < 0.005\n  - Compute exact C5 value using np.correlate(mode='full') \n- Returns (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n- Includes fallback if parent solution is available",
